---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Example from '../components/Example.astro';

const SITE = 'https://lepidus-design-system-button.vercel.app';

const installCdn = `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
<head>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="${SITE}/v1/lepidus.css" />
</head>
<body>
  <!-- your page -->
  <script src="${SITE}/v1/lepidus.js" defer></script>
</body>
</html>`;

const reactQuickstart = `import '@lepidus/tokens/build/css/tokens.css';
import { Button, Badge } from '@lepidus/react';

export function ReportToolbar() {
  return (
    <>
      <Button variant="primary">Run scan</Button>
      <Badge variant="success">14 servers healthy</Badge>
    </>
  );
}`;
---

<Page
  title="Get started"
  eyebrow="Start here"
  lede="Your first on-brand Lepide screen takes about five minutes: one stylesheet, copy-paste markup, three themes for free. Pick your path."
>
  <ul class="path-grid" aria-label="Choose your path">
    <li>
      <a class="path-card" href="#start">
        <span class="path-card__icon" style="background:#e9f0ff" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1c4599" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m8 7-5 5 5 5M16 7l5 5-5 5"/></svg>
        </span>
        <span>
          <strong>I write HTML/CSS/JS</strong>
          <small>The default — start below</small>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a class="path-card" href="#react">
        <span class="path-card__icon" style="background:#e4f3ec" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#176639" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2.2"/><ellipse cx="12" cy="12" rx="10" ry="4.2"/><ellipse cx="12" cy="12" rx="10" ry="4.2" transform="rotate(60 12 12)"/><ellipse cx="12" cy="12" rx="10" ry="4.2" transform="rotate(120 12 12)"/></svg>
        </span>
        <span>
          <strong>My project is React</strong>
          <small>Same system, typed</small>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a class="path-card" href="/foundations/">
        <span class="path-card__icon" style="background:#fdf1e2" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#8a5b00" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 19a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 2.4-2.4h4.2A2.4 2.4 0 0 0 21 14.2V6a2 2 0 0 0-2-2h-5.5"/><path d="M12 19a2.4 2.4 0 0 0-2.4-2.4H5.4A2.4 2.4 0 0 1 3 14.2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h5.5"/></svg>
        </span>
        <span>
          <strong>I design</strong>
          <small>Foundations, themes, tokens</small>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a class="path-card" href="/ai/">
        <span class="path-card__icon" style="background:#f0ebfa" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#6d5bd0" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3v2M12 19v2M3 12h2M19 12h2M5.6 5.6l1.4 1.4M17 17l1.4 1.4M18.4 5.6 17 7M7 17l-1.4 1.4"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/></svg>
        </span>
        <span>
          <strong>I use an AI assistant</strong>
          <small>Set it up in five minutes</small>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="step" id="start"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>Install and ship your first component</h2>
  <p>
    The easiest way — link the two hosted files. Nothing to download, copy, or build;
    <code>lepidus.css</code> carries the tokens and every component, and
    <code>lepidus.js</code> (4&nbsp;KB) adds behavior to the interactive ones. The
    <code>/v1/</code> path stays stable, so your pages don't break when the system updates.
  </p>
  <Code code={installCdn} lang="html" theme="github-dark" />
  <Callout variant="info" title="Prefer to vendor the files?">
    <p>You can also copy <code>lepidus.css</code> and <code>lepidus.js</code> from
    <code>packages/css/dist/</code> into your own project and link them with relative paths —
    handy for offline or air-gapped environments. React teams install the packages instead
    (step&nbsp;3).</p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Now copy markup from any component's Code tab and paste. Here's an audit-report toolbar from
    <a href="/components/button/code/">Button</a> and <a href="/components/badge/code/">Badge</a> —
    live below, so hover and tab through it:
  </p>
  <Example snippet="get-started/first-component" title="A report toolbar — your first paste" story="actions-button--primary" />
  <p>
    Classes follow one rule: block (<code>lp-button</code>), modifier
    (<code>lp-button--primary</code>), element (<code>lp-button__icon-left</code>). If a class
    isn't in the docs, it doesn't exist — the <a href="/components/">component pages</a> are the
    complete vocabulary.
  </p>

  <h2 class="step"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>Theming and behavior come for free</h2>
  <p>
    Press <strong>Dark</strong> in the example above — that's the whole theming feature. Every
    color is a token that re-resolves when <code>data-theme</code> changes on
    <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>: no second stylesheet, nothing to maintain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Interactive components wire themselves up: write the documented markup and add a
    <code>data-lp-*</code> attribute (or one <code>Lepidus.*</code> call), and
    <code>lepidus.js</code> handles focus traps, arrow keys, and Escape. Try it — this button
    raises a real toast:
  </p>
  <Example snippet="get-started/interactive" title="Behavior from lepidus.js — no code written" height={210} story="feedback-toast--info" />
  <Callout variant="info" title="The one rule that makes all of this work">
    <p>Never hardcode a color, size, or radius — use the <a href="/tokens/">token variables</a>
    (<code>var(--component-*)</code> first, then <code>var(--color-*)</code> /
    <code>var(--space-*)</code> / <code>var(--radius-*)</code>). It's enforced in CI, and it's
    the reason the three themes work for free. Hardcode one hex value and dark mode breaks
    exactly there.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 class="step" id="react"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>On React? The same system, typed</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@lepidus/react</code> renders pixel-identically to the plain-CSS classes — both come
    from the same source. Props are documented on every component's Specifications tab and
    can't drift from the code.
  </p>
  <Code code={reactQuickstart} lang="tsx" theme="github-dark" />
  <p>
    The <a href="/storybook/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storybook ↗</a> is the interactive
    workshop behind this whole site — every state of every component, across all three themes
    and RTL.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul class="tile-grid">
    <li><a class="tile" href="/components/"><p class="tile__title">Components</p><p class="tile__desc">44 documented components — usage, code, specifications, accessibility.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="tile" href="/tokens/"><p class="tile__title">Design tokens</p><p class="tile__desc">Every variable with per-theme values, click-to-copy.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="tile" href="/foundations/"><p class="tile__title">Foundations</p><p class="tile__desc">Typography, theming, spacing, and the token architecture.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="tile" href="/storybook/" target="_blank"><p class="tile__title">Storybook ↗</p><p class="tile__desc">The interactive workshop behind every example on this site.</p></a></li>
  </ul>
</Page>
